{"name":"Green Energy","hex":"#80905f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green energy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A nonpolluting source of energy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green_energy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sustainable energy","blocks":[{"text":"Energy is sustainable if it \"meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\" Definitions of sustainable energy usually look at its effects on the environment, the economy, and society. These impacts range from greenhouse gas emissions and air pollution to energy poverty and toxic waste. Renewable energy sources such as wind, hydro, solar, and geothermal energy can cause environmental damage but are generally far more sustainable than fossil fuel sources."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Andasol_Guadix_4.jpg/330px-Andasol_Guadix_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sustainable_energy","wikidata":"Q1962383"},{"title":"Energy","blocks":[{"text":"Energy is the quantitative property that is transferred to a body or to a physical system, recognizable in the capacity to do work and in the form of heat and light. Energy is a conserved quantity – the law of conservation of energy states that the total energy of an isolated system remains constant, although it can be converted in form. The unit of measurement for energy in the International System of Units (SI) is the joule (J)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Plasma_globe_60th.jpg/330px-Plasma_globe_60th.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy","wikidata":"Q11379"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Darling_Wind_Farm.jpg/500px-Darling_Wind_Farm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Darling_Wind_Farm.jpg/1280px-Darling_Wind_Farm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Darling_Wind_Farm.jpg/500px-Darling_Wind_Farm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADarling_Wind_Farm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/R151_Arriving_Buona_Vista_MRT_Station.jpg/500px-R151_Arriving_Buona_Vista_MRT_Station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/